<p>Therefore, to invert a binary tree, we <strong>swap the left and right c=
hildren of every node</strong>. Now, let=E2=80=99s explore a tree traversal=
 algorithm that allows us to do this.</p>
<p><strong>Depth-first search</strong><br>
Our strategy is to visit every node in the binary tree and swap its left an=
d right children. There=E2=80=99s no particular order in which we need to v=
isit the nodes. This means we can employ any tree traversal algorithm, as l=
ong as every node is visited.</p>
<p>Let=E2=80=99s tackle this problem recursively using DFS. After swapping =
the left and right children of the root node, we recursively call our inver=
t function on the left and right children to invert their subtrees as well.=
</p>
